post_install() {
    cat << 'MSG'
==> RABA is installed and does nothing yet. On purpose: a package that puts
    itself in your boot path without being asked is not a good neighbour.

    1. Add `raba` to HOOKS in /etc/mkinitcpio.conf, after `encrypt` if you use
       disk encryption:

           HOOKS=(base udev autodetect modconf kms keyboard block encrypt raba filesystems fsck)

    2. Rebuild the initramfs:

           sudo mkinitcpio -P

    3. Arrange for something to end the splash. The packaged units are the
       easy answer:

           sudo systemctl enable raba-quit.service raba-quit-wait.service

    `raba-setup` prints all of this for your machine, and `raba-setup --apply`
    does what it safely can. `raba --check` says what the configuration
    resolves to.

==> If Plymouth is installed and enabled, RABA will see it at boot, log the
    reason and exit. Two splashes on one terminal is a black screen. Nothing is
    removed for you.

==> Test in a virtual machine before rebooting a machine you need.
MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat << 'MSG'
==> The initramfs carries a copy of /etc/raba/raba.conf and of your theme.
    After an upgrade that changes either, rebuild it:

        sudo mkinitcpio -P

    `raba-setup --check` says whether the image is current.
MSG
}

post_remove() {
    cat << 'MSG'
==> Remove `raba` from HOOKS in /etc/mkinitcpio.conf and rebuild the initramfs:

        sudo mkinitcpio -P

    The hook is gone, so an image built with it still in HOOKS simply skips it,
    but leaving it there is untidy.
MSG
}
